{"reference":"Hosea 12","book":"Hosea","chapter":12,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading Hosea’s Political and Historical References","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter’s references to Assyria, Egypt, agreements, wealth, oppression, and judgment belong to the northern kingdom’s later imperial crisis. Hosea sets these present pressures alongside Israel’s remembered story: Jacob, the exodus, life in tents, and prophetic guidance. This reuse of earlier traditions diagnoses a present failure of identity and calls for change. The critique of alliances and rulers addresses destructive power, elite exploitation, war, and misplaced security; it should not be turned into a prescription for a modern party or state policy.","verse_refs":["Hosea 12:1-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hosea_012_186e677f39b703dbac3173a7"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}